.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
netstat
- show the network card's listening and connected sockets
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
netstat
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
netstat opens the card's clone node, /dev/net0, and asks the card for
its socket table.  It prints one line for every socket that is
listening or connected:
.sp
.nf
.in +4
Proto Local          Foreign         State
tcp   *.514           *.*             LISTEN
tcp   *.514           127.0.0.1.40001 ESTABLISHED
.in -4
.fi
.sp
The local address is the port the socket is bound to; the card owns the
interface address, and a star stands for it.  The foreign address is the
peer's dotted quad and port, or *.* when there is no peer.  State is
LISTEN for a server waiting for a connection and ESTABLISHED for a
connection the card has accepted, or a client's connection.
.sp
The table is the card's, not the kernel's: a socket belongs to whatever
process opened it, and only the card knows all of them.  A connection
that has arrived but has not been accepted yet is still the listener's
(the card is holding it), so it appears as a LISTEN line with no
foreign address.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
